course: euphemisms
lesson: 10
title: Soft words for hard things: weight and pregnancy and birth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Talking about someone's weight can hurt, so people reach for softer words than "fat".
explain: Some are kind ("curvy", "cuddly"), some are old-fashioned ("portly", "stout"), some are jokes ("love handles", "dad bod"), and shops use labels like "husky" for boys' sizes.
explain: Old soft words for having a baby stay too: "with child" means pregnant, and "a happy event" is a birth.
explain: Vivid example: a granny says "Your uncle's a bit well-upholstered these days" — he is not a sofa; she means he has got fat.
explain: Remember that even soft words about weight can still hurt, so it is kinder not to comment on people's bodies at all.
fact: "Big-boned" is an everyday soft way to say fat; bones are rarely the real reason.
fact: "Curvy" usually means a woman is plump, with a rounded shape.
fact: "Cuddly" said of a person often means fat, in a warm way.
fact: "Love handles" is a joking name for fat around the waist.
fact: "Well-padded" is a joking way to say fat.
fact: "Portly" is an old-fashioned word for a fat man.
fact: "Stout" is an old-fashioned word for fat.
fact: "Carrying a few extra pounds" is a polite way to say overweight.
fact: "Heavyset" is an American way to say fat or heavily built.
fact: "Rubenesque" means plump and attractive, after the painter Rubens, who painted round women.
fact: "Dad bod" is slang for a soft, out-of-shape male body.
fact: "On the heavy side" is a polite way to say fat.
fact: "Well-upholstered" is a joking British way to say fat, as if the person were a padded sofa.
fact: "Pleasingly plump" is an old-fashioned way to say fat in a nice-looking way.
fact: "A weight problem" usually means obesity, being very overweight.
fact: "Chunky" often means fat, especially of a child.
fact: "Husky" is an American clothing size for large or fat boys.
fact: "With child" is an old-fashioned way to say pregnant.
fact: "A visit from the stork" is a joking way to say a baby is born; children were told storks bring babies.
fact: "A happy event" is a polite British way to talk about the birth of a baby.
exercise: Someone says "I'm not fat, I'm just big-boned." What do they really mean? | They are fat | Their bones are broken | They have a big skeleton | They are tall
exercise: A fashion magazine calls a woman "curvy". What does it mean? | She is plump with a rounded shape | She likes winding roads | She has curly hair | She is very thin
exercise: Gran calls Grandpa "my cuddly bear". What does it suggest about him? | He is fat in a warm way | He is a toy bear | He is very hairy | He gives hugs at bedtime only
exercise: Dad pinches his waist: "Look at these love handles!" What is he showing? | Fat around his waist | Handles on a mug | Scars from an operation | His belt buckle
exercise: Someone jokes that a man is "well-padded". What do they mean? | He is fat | He wears a padded coat | He plays rugby with pads | He is cosy
exercise: An old book describes "a portly gentleman". What was he like? | Fat | Working at a port | Polite | Carrying a suitcase
exercise: In an old story, "a stout woman" opened the door. What was she like? | Fat | Brave | Drinking beer | Very strong-willed
exercise: The doctor says "You're carrying a few extra pounds." What does he mean? | You are overweight | You have coins in your pocket | Your bag is heavy | You owe money
exercise: An American report describes "a heavyset man". What was he like? | Fat or heavily built | Tired and sleepy | Carrying heavy boxes | Very serious
exercise: An art guide calls a painted woman "Rubenesque". What does it mean? | Plump and attractive | Red-haired and pale-skinned | Painted in red | Very rich
exercise: A magazine says "The dad bod is in fashion." What is a dad bod? | A soft, out-of-shape male body | A muscly fit body | A body of a young father | A group of dads
exercise: A form says the patient is "on the heavy side". What does it mean? | He is fat | He sits on the left | He lifts weights | He is tall
exercise: A Brit jokes "Uncle Jim is a bit well-upholstered." What is she saying? | Jim is fat | Jim makes sofas | Jim is smart | Jim has a new car seat
exercise: An old advert praises a "pleasingly plump" cook. What is she like? | Fat in a nice-looking way | Very good at cooking | Happy all the time | Short and quick
exercise: A health leaflet talks about people "with a weight problem". What problem is it? | Being very overweight | Being bad at lifting | Being too weak | Being bad at maths
exercise: Grandma says "What a chunky little boy!" What does she mean? | He is fat | He is very strong | He eats chunky soup | He is tall
exercise: An American store sells boys' jeans in "husky" sizes. Who are they for? | Large or fat boys | Boys who own husky dogs | Boys with deep voices | Boys who play in snow
exercise: In an old book, "the queen was with child". What does it mean? | She was pregnant | She was holding a baby | She was babysitting | She was with her daughter
exercise: An old card says "Expecting a visit from the stork!" What is going to happen? | A baby will be born | A bird will come to the garden | A doctor will visit | A family will go to the zoo
exercise: A British notice says "The Smiths look forward to a happy event in May." What will happen? | A baby will be born | A wedding party | A birthday party | A holiday
test: Kid: "he's not fat he's big-boned, that's what his mum says lol" — what is the kid hinting? | The boy is fat | The boy has strong bones | The boy is tall | The boy's mum is a doctor
test: A sitcom line: "Me? I've got a dad bod, mate, it's all pizza." What does he mean? | His body is soft and out of shape | He has just become a dad | He is a body builder | He works in a pizza shop
test: News: "Witnesses describe the suspect as a heavyset man in his forties." What did the suspect look like? | Fat or heavily built | Carrying heavy bags of shopping | Very tired | Very short
test: A clothes shop label: "Husky fit – boys 8–16". What is it? | A large size for big boys | A jumper for husky dogs | Snow clothes | A fit for thin boys
test: Grandpa pats his belly: "Bit of extra padding for winter, eh!" What is he joking about? | His fat belly | His winter coat | His pillow | A heater
test: An old novel: "Mrs Bennet's sister was a stout, cheerful woman." What does "stout" say? | She was fat | She was brave | She was cross | She was tall
test: The doctor tells Dad: "You're carrying a few extra pounds — more walking, less beer." What is the doctor saying? | Dad is overweight | Dad has too much money | Dad owes money | Dad walks too much
test: Ella reckons "love handles" are handles on a heart-shaped mug. Is she right? | No, it means fat around the waist | Yes, they are on mugs | Yes, they are on door knobs | No, it means holding hands
test: Royal news from 1850: "Her Majesty is with child." What is the news? | The queen is pregnant | The queen adopted a child | The queen is visiting a school | The queen is ill
test: A text from Auntie: "guess who's getting a visit from the stork 👶🎉" What is she announcing? | A baby is coming | A bird nest on the roof | A trip to the zoo | A new pet
test: Rude brother: "shift over, you're well-upholstered, you take up the whole sofa" — what is he saying? | His sister is fat | His sister bought a new sofa | His sister has many cushions | His sister is lazy
test: In an art gallery: "The painting shows Rubenesque women dancing." What do the women look like? | Plump and attractive | Thin and pale | Small and short | Old and wrinkled
test: A magazine headline: "Curvy is the new beautiful." What kind of body is it praising? | A plump, rounded shape | A thin straight shape | A muscly shape | A tall shape
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the archaic expression "with child". | Pregnant | Accompanied by an infant | Engaged in the care of children | Possessing children
exam: It is given that a witness describes a person as "portly". Ascertain the physical characteristic so described. | Corpulence; being fat | Employment at a harbour | Dignified manners | Considerable height
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the phrase "a happy event" in British usage. | The birth of a baby | A wedding ceremony | A birthday celebration | A successful examination
exam: Whereas a retailer in the United States classifies garments for boys as "husky", determine which of the following most accurately characterises the said classification. | A size for large or heavy boys | A range for winter weather | A style for outdoor sport | A collection featuring dog motifs
